    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "<color=#8B0000><size=160%><b>The Raven at the Door</b></size></color>\n\nAt
    midnight, someone knocked upon my door.\n\nThree times.\n\nI opened it, but no
    one stood outside.\n\nOnly a raven sat upon the railing, its feathers wet with
    rain.\n\nIt looked at me with eyes too bright for any living bird.\n\nThen, in
    a voice like a buried bell, it whispered:\n\n\"He remembers.\"\n\nI closed the
    door at once.\n\nBut the knocking continued from inside the room.\n\n<page><color=#4B0082><size=160%><b>The
    Candle in the Mirror</b></size></color>\n\nI lit one candle before the mirror.\n\nIn
    the glass, there were two.\n\nI turned around, but behind me the room was dark.\n\nThe
    second flame burned only in the reflection.\n\nThen a pale hand appeared beside
    it.\n\nSlowly, the hand reached forward from inside the mirror.\n\nBefore I could
    move, it pressed one finger to the glass and wrote:\n\n\"Your turn.\"\n\n<page><color=#003366><size=160%><b>The
    House Beneath the Rain</b></size></color>\n\nThe old house had no windows, yet
    every night a light shone from within.\n\nI entered during a storm, carrying
    only a lantern.\n\nInside, the floor was dry, but I heard rain falling somewhere
    below.\n\nBeneath the cellar stairs, I found a locked wooden door.\n\nFrom behind
    it came the sound of thunder.\n\nThen a child\u2019s voice whispered:\n\n\"Do
    not open it. The sky is buried here.\"\n\n<page><color=#006400><size=160%><b>The
    Portrait That Blinked</b></size></color>\n\nThe portrait hung above the fireplace
    for one hundred years.\n\nEveryone said the woman in it had died smiling.\n\nBut
    when I looked closely, her smile faded.\n\nI stepped back.\n\nThe painted eyes
    blinked.\n\nThen the woman raised one painted finger and pointed behind me.\n\nI
    turned and saw nothing.\n\nWhen I looked again, the portrait was empty.\n\n<page><color=#B8860B><size=160%><b>The
    Last Bell</b></size></color>\n\nThe chapel bell had not rung since the village
    was abandoned.\n\nYet at dawn, it sounded once.\n\nI climbed the tower to see
    who had pulled the rope.\n\nNo one was there.\n\nOnly a shadow hung where the
    bell should have been.\n\nIt swayed gently in the cold air.\n\nThen the bell
    rang again.\n\nThis time, the sound came from inside my chest."
